    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d523">

                <pb facs="0551" n="433" xml:id="pg433"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.512/3522</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-08-27">August 27, 1929</date>.<lb/>[Received September
                        3.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1832</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Embassy’s
                    despatch No. 1799 of August 13, 1929,<anchor n="97" xml:id="d523-f1"/> relative
                    to the attitude of the Mexican Government, as reported in the press, toward the
                    collection of taxes which have already been paid to rebel authorities in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> possession of given territory. I now have
                    the honor to enclose herewith a translation of a decree of the Ministry of
                    Finance and Public Credit, published in the <hi rendition="#italic">Diario
                        Oficial</hi> of August 23, 1929, No. 44, Vol. LV.<anchor n="97"
                        xml:id="d523-f2"/> By this measure the decree of March 5, 1929, which
                    declared invalid the payment of fiscal credits made to rebels, is revoked.
                    Article 2 of the decree provides that payments which were made to offices of the
                    Federal Government during the time of the rebellion, and which payments also
                    were made to rebel elements, will not be returned to the payers. The purpose of
                    the decree seems to be to place Mexican nationals on as favorable a footing in
                    regard to this matter as are foreign persons under rules of international
                    law.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herschel V.
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
